(# The preamble both standalone documents share, and the reason it is one file.

`mathpage.jtex` sets every formula of an HTML page; `standalone.jtex` sets one drawing for a
PDF or an SVG. They want the same 26 packages, the same babel options, the same five inputs and
the same chemfig and TikZ setup, because what they produce has to match what the booklet
produces -- the web maths beside the printed maths, the standalone drawing beside the one in the
page.

It was two copies of 73 identical lines, held together by a comment in each saying "a package
added there belongs here too". That comment was the whole of what kept them equal, and a comment
cannot fail a build.

**Included by Jinja rather than \input by LaTeX**, because of one line: the i18n table below is
named through a tag, and that tag has to be resolved before TeX ever sees the file. An \input
would hand LaTeX the tag itself. A Jinja include splices the text in instead, which is why the
rendered .tex is byte-for-byte what it was before this file existed -- and why this explanation
is a Jinja comment: a LaTeX one would reach every rendered document and move exactly the bytes
the extraction must not move. #)

\RequirePackage[table]{xcolor}
\RequirePackage{tikz}

\RequirePackage{amsmath}
\RequirePackage{amssymb}
\RequirePackage{mleftright}
\RequirePackage{siunitx}
\RequirePackage[weather]{ifsym}
\RequirePackage{nicematrix}

\RequirePackage{wasysym}                                    % extra symbols for planets, astrology, ...
\RequirePackage{mathtools}
\RequirePackage[version=4]{mhchem}                          % simple chemical formulae
% **`chemformula` is loaded here and deliberately not in `dgs.cls`**, and the two are right to
% differ. `chem/03/eschenmoser/mechanizmus.tikz` and `chem/03/fluoresceín/reakcia.tikz` write
% `\fplus` and `\fminus`, which are chemformula's; they are drawn by *this* path and reach the
% booklet as finished PDFs, so the class never needs it and never loads it.
%
% It is also why the babel list below is this document's own rather than the class's: chemformula
% and babel's `russian` both define `\ch`, which is what `dgs.cls:85`'s `\chemsetup{formula=mhchem}`
% exists to dodge and what these templates dodge by not loading Russian at all.
\RequirePackage{chemformula}
\RequirePackage{chemmacros}
\RequirePackage{chemfig}                                    % complex chemical formulae

\RequirePackage[all]{nowidow}                               % prevent widow and orphan lines
\RequirePackage[minionint, mathlf]{MinionPro}
\RequirePackage[MnSymbol]{mathspec}                         % includes fontspec
\RequirePackage[f]{esvect}
\RequirePackage{accents}
\RequirePackage{caption}
\RequirePackage{cleveref}

% **Not `core/latex/babel.tex`, and not by oversight.** These documents load `chemformula`
% (above) and the class does not; chemformula and babel's `russian` both define `\ch`, so
% taking the class's nine languages here is `Command \ch already defined` at
% `\begin{document}`. A standalone document sets one drawing or one formula and needs no
% Russian hyphenation to do it.
%
% `english` is last on purpose: babel makes the **last** option the main language, and this list
% used to end in `spanish` while the booklet's ended in `english`. Nothing has ever shown it,
% because `build/core/i18n/<lang>.tex` opens with `\selectlanguage` and every document inputs it
% a few lines below -- but a document that does not would have been set in Spanish.
\RequirePackage[
    shorthands=off,
    slovak,
    czech,
    german,
    french,
    polish,
    spanish,
    english,
]{babel}                                                    % multi-language support
\RequirePackage[autostyle]{csquotes}                        % proper localized quotation marks

\input{core/latex/fonts.tex}
\input{core/latex/utilities.tex}
\input{core/latex/math.tex}
\input{core/latex/symbols.tex}
\input{core/latex/siunitx.tex}

\input{build/core/i18n/(* lang *).tex}

\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\usetikzlibrary{matrix, backgrounds, fit, positioning}

\setchemfig{
    atom sep=7mm,
    bond style={line width=1pt},
    bond join=true,
    schemestart code=\chemnameinit{},
}
\tikzset{
  over line/.style={
    white,
    double=black,
    double distance=\the\pgflinewidth,
    line width=1.5pt,
  },
  under line/.style={
    decoration={
      markings,
      mark connection node=mid node,
      mark=at position #1 with {}
      {\node[transform shape,minimum size=3pt] (mid node) {};}
    },
    decorate
  }
}

(# `trim_blocks` eats the newline after the include tag, so this file ends with the blank
line the templates expect between the preamble and what follows it. Do not strip it. #)
